==============================================================================
G204 -- THE SCREENED PARENT: DOES THE BIHARMONIC k^4 SCREEN THE GHOST?
==============================================================================
  Lam^4 = 5.2532e-10 J/m^3    ceiling (Park 2026) = 5.20e-27 s^-2
  a_0: canonical 9.3619e-11 / alternative 1.1279e-10 m/s^2
  canonical    a_0=9.3619e-11  r_M(Sun) = 7960 AU = 0.0386 pc   xi/r_M = 1.166
  alternative  a_0=1.1279e-10  r_M(Sun) = 7252 AU = 0.0352 pc   xi/r_M = 1.280
  xi = 0.0450 pc = 9282 AU   (H004's f30 Cassini floor)

==============================================================================
PART (a) -- THE GHOST TEST: THE HESSIAN OF THE COMBINED OPERATOR
==============================================================================

  Static Hamiltonian density:   H = -Lam^4 f(K) + (xi^2/2)(D^2 phi)^2
  Second variation around a background with u^2 = K (u = |Dphi|/(2 a0) on the
  H045 calibration), background gradient along x-hat:

    delta^2 H = -Lam^4 f'(u) (D psi)^2/a0^2
                - 2 Lam^4 f''(u) (Dphi0 . Dpsi)^2/a0^4
                + xi^2 (D^2 psi)^2

  In Fourier (k, th = angle to the background gradient):

    Q(k,th) = -(f'(u) + 2 f''(u) u^2 cos^2 th) k^2 + xi^2 k^4           (scaled)

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
(a1) THE PER-MODE FORM: the wrong-sign k^2 term is untouched at k->0
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      u0 =  0.05:  f' = mu_2 =  0.0930 > 0,   k^2-coeff at th=0: -( 0.1794) < 0
      u0 =  0.10:  f' = mu_2 =  0.1736 > 0,   k^2-coeff at th=0: -( 0.3238) < 0
      u0 =  0.50:  f' = mu_2 =  0.5556 > 0,   k^2-coeff at th=0: -( 0.8519) < 0
      u0 =  0.90:  f' = mu_2 =  0.7230 > 0,   k^2-coeff at th=0: -( 0.9854) < 0
  [PASS] A1 [IR GHOST SURVIVES] for every representative deep config the
      k^2 coefficient is -(f' + 2 f'' u^2) < 0, so Q(k) ~ -c1 k^2 < 0
      as k -> 0 at EVERY xi: the biharmonic adds k^4, which is
      subdominant at long wavelengths (an IR-irrelevant operator)
         measured: u0=0.05: c1=0.1794; u0=0.1: c1=0.3238; u0=0.5: c1=0.8519; u0=0.9: c1=0.9854
         threshold: c1 > 0 at every u0 >= 0 (f' > 0, f'' u^2 > 0 for u > 0)
         The ghost lives at k -> 0 where xi^2 k^4 / c1 k^2 -> 0.  Energy per
         mode -c1 k^2 |psi_k|^2 -> -inf by scaling |psi_k|: unbounded
         below for every finite xi.  xi_c as a healing threshold
         would be INFINITE in the IR sector.
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
(a2) THE 3x3 KINETIC MATRIX on {1, x, x^2} (exact, box [-L/2,L/2]^3)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Probe basis:  psi_1 = 1 (const),  psi_2 = x (affine),  psi_3 = x^2 (curved);
  box scale L = r_M(Sun) ~ 8000 AU.  Grams (L = 1):  P_22 = 1 (gradient),
  P_33 = 1/3, B_33 = 4 (biharmonic); all other entries zero by symmetry.
  With c = f'(u0) + 2 f''(u0) u0^2  and  s = xi/L:

      M(xi) = -c diag(0, 1, 1/3)  +  xi^2 diag(0, 0, 4)     (L = 1, scaled)

  Eigenvalues:  lambda_1 = 0           (shift-symmetric direction ~ 1)
                lambda_2 = -c          (AFFINE direction: xi-INDEPENDENT)
                lambda_3 = -c/3 + 4 s^2 (curved: healed above xi_c3)

      u0 = 0.05:  lambda_2 = -0.1794 (xi-independent), lambda_3(xi=0.045pc) =   5.3793 (healed: yes),  xi_c3 = 0.0047 pc
      u0 = 0.10:  lambda_2 = -0.3238 (xi-independent), lambda_3(xi=0.045pc) =   5.3312 (healed: yes),  xi_c3 = 0.0063 pc
      u0 = 0.90:  lambda_2 = -0.9854 (xi-independent), lambda_3(xi=0.045pc) =   5.1106 (healed: yes),  xi_c3 = 0.0111 pc
  [PASS] A2 [THE AFFINE KERNEL] the affine mode {a + b.x} has Delta^2 w = 0
      EXACTLY (it lies in the biharmonic's kernel), so its Hessian
      eigenvalue is lambda_2 = -c < 0 at EVERY xi: this ghost channel
      can never be healed by any finite xi (xi_c2 = infinity)
         measured: u0=0.05: lambda_2=-0.1794, xi_c2=inf; u0=0.1: lambda_2=-0.3238, xi_c2=inf; u0=0.9: lambda_2=-0.9854, xi_c2=inf
         threshold: lambda_2 < 0 for every xi and every u0
         The curved sector {x^2} IS healed above xi_c3 ~ 0.005-0.011 pc -- this
         is the part of the spectrum the k^4 operator renormalizes.  But
         the Hessian stays INDEFINITE because the affine direction is
         in the kernel of Delta^2 and in the negative part of the
         gradient energy.  One healthy-sounding sector is not health.
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
(a3) THE SPECTRAL RADIAL HESSIAN on {r^n, n=-3..5}: lambda_min vs xi
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  On the power basis {r^n} the laplacian is EXACT:  Delta r^n = n(n+1) r^{n-2},
  so the biharmonic Gram has no truncation error and its kernel members are
  {r^0, r^{-1}} -- Delta of both is exactly zero.  The r^-1 member lies in
  the negative gradient-energy sector:  P(1/r) = int (d(1/r)/dr)^2 r^2 dr > 0,
  so its Hessian eigenvalue is  -c P(1/r) < 0  at EVERY xi.  The constant
  r^0 is the shift symmetry (eigenvalue exactly 0 in every channel).
  Window [1e-3, 1e3] x r_M(Sun);  s = xi/r_M(Sun) dimensionless.
      kernel member r^-1:  P(1/r) = 1.0000e+03 > 0,  B(1/r) = 0.000e+00 (exact kernel of the biharmonic)
      u0 = 0.1:  Rayleigh bound on the 1/r member = -3.2382e+02 (s=1e-6) .. -3.2382e+02 (s=1e6), negative at all 25 s: True;  small-s window: strongest negative in [-5.757e+24, -5.757e+24]
  [PASS] A3 [0.1] INDEFINITE AT EVERY xi: the r^-1 member is an exact
      kernel of the biharmonic (B row identically zero) and carries
      the negative gradient energy: v^T M(s) v = -c1 P(1/r) < 0 for
      every finite s = xi/r_M(Sun) (lambda_min <= that < 0)
         measured: Rayleigh bound negative at all 25 s in [1e-6, 1e6]: True; small-s window: negative mode present at all 9 s
         threshold: lambda_min < 0 for xi in [1e-6, 1e12] pc (all finite xi)
         The k^4 term pushes high-k/curved modes positive (lambda_3 sector)
         but the kernel-sector eigenvalue saturates at -c*lambda_A
         < 0: unbounded below persists.  xi_c does not exist.
         V1 numbers: P_X = -f' = -mu_2(u0) < 0 UNCHANGED by the
         biharmonic (space-only operator); G^00 = -f' < 0; the
         wrong-sign gradient energy is exactly the affine sector.
      u0 = 0.9:  Rayleigh bound on the 1/r member = -9.8542e+02 (s=1e-6) .. -9.8542e+02 (s=1e6), negative at all 25 s: True;  small-s window: strongest negative in [-1.752e+25, -1.752e+25]
  [PASS] A3 [0.9] INDEFINITE AT EVERY xi: the r^-1 member is an exact
      kernel of the biharmonic (B row identically zero) and carries
      the negative gradient energy: v^T M(s) v = -c1 P(1/r) < 0 for
      every finite s = xi/r_M(Sun) (lambda_min <= that < 0)
         measured: Rayleigh bound negative at all 25 s in [1e-6, 1e6]: True; small-s window: negative mode present at all 9 s
         threshold: lambda_min < 0 for xi in [1e-6, 1e12] pc (all finite xi)
         The k^4 term pushes high-k/curved modes positive (lambda_3 sector)
         but the kernel-sector eigenvalue saturates at -c*lambda_A
         < 0: unbounded below persists.  xi_c does not exist.
         V1 numbers: P_X = -f' = -mu_2(u0) < 0 UNCHANGED by the
         biharmonic (space-only operator); G^00 = -f' < 0; the
         wrong-sign gradient energy is exactly the affine sector.
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
(a4) THE TIME-KINETIC COEFFICIENT: G^00 = -f' is INVARIANT
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  The biharmonic term is built from SPATIAL derivatives only
  (H004's own F1: homogeneous phi => D^2 phi = 0 identically).  The
  perturbation's time-kinetic coefficient comes from P_X only:

        L2 = (1/2) G^{mn} d_m psi d_n psi,   G^00 = -f' = -mu_2(u).

  The k^4 term adds no X-dependence, so P_X = -mu_2 < 0 is IDENTICALLY
  unchanged for every xi: the ghost is invariant under the 'screening'.
  This closes the loop with H045 C1 without any numeric approximation.
  [PASS] A4 [P_X INVARIANT] P_X = -f'(K) = -mu_2(u) < 0 at every K > 0 and
      every xi: the biharmonic term does not enter P(X) at all
      (it is a higher-derivative spatial term, X-independent)
         measured: P_X(u=0.1) = -0.1736, P_X(u=1.0) = -0.7500, independent of xi by construction
         threshold: P_X < 0 unchanged
         Any candidate that would heal the ghost must change P(X); the k^4
         operator cannot.  THE SCREENED PARENT DOES NOT SCREEN THE
         GHOST -- this is the decisive (a) verdict.

==============================================================================
PART (b) -- THE CASSINI GATE: THE G03 MACHINERY, REUSED
==============================================================================
  The biharmonic propagator phi = -GM(1 - e^{-r/xi})/r has the Fourier
  form 1/[k^2 (1 + xi^2 k^2)]  -- the isotropic Helmholtz SINGLE filter of
  the G03 44-solve scan (g03_candidate1_gates_v2.out), whose registered
  result is quoted verbatim and then re-derived for xi = 0.045 pc:

      xi = 0.000 pc : 6.44x  (alt 7.63x)      |Q2|/ceiling, both footings
      xi = 0.030 pc : 6.44x  (alt 7.64x)
      xi = 0.050 pc : 6.45x  (alt 7.65x)
      xi = 0.100 pc : 6.49x  (alt 7.65x)      min over the scan: 6.18x (1-sig)

  MONOTONE NON-IMPROVEMENT, and the smooth-shell lemma: an isotropic radial
  smoothing -- Helmholtz, Gaussian, single or double -- preserves the l=2
  moment of the mu-transition shell, because the quadrupole is generated by
  the shell's ANGULAR structure.  (1-e^{-r/xi})/r is exactly that class.

